 "caveats": [
  "GIBS layers are visualisations: class values are exact palette decodes, but geometry is nearest-neighbour resampled from native 30 m (DSWx) / 500 m (MODIS) to the WMS grid. Cell fractions at 0.01 deg are therefore approximate area fractions.",
  "When several DSWx granules overlap on one day, GIBS composites them; the granule actually displayed per pixel is not recorded here. Granule-level provenance requires the CMR/ASF GeoTIFFs (fetch_reference.py --cmr lists them).",
  "DSWx-S1 dates are 1 day before NISAR dates (Sentinel-1 overpass); 2026-06-05 has no coverage in the AOI, so the NISAR 2026-06-20 baseline has no DSWx-S1 counterpart; 2026-08-06 matches the missing NISAR 2026-08-07 slot; 2026-10-29 has no NISAR counterpart.",
  "DSWx-HLS is optical: it cannot see water beneath emergent vegetation (papyrus, Typha, flooded grassland). Absence of optical water is not evidence that the surface beneath the canopy is dry.",
  "DSWx-S1 'inundated vegetation' is itself a C-band backscatter-derived classification with its own errors; it is an independent sensor, not ground truth.",
  "MODIS IGBP at 500 m is coarse; the region labels are used only to stratify, never as truth for NISAR pixel classes."
 ]
}